From d87601319b08a45d7685d33c95b58123ce5a947a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: coppock <patrick@gpuos.ai>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:58:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Dual priority-stream execution: two engines, one process,
 shared weights

Each TP worker holds ONE copy of the weights and TWO model-runner states: a
LATENCY role (high-priority CUDA stream, small KV reservation) and a THROUGHPUT
role (default stream, the rest of the pool). EngineCore runs two schedulers over
the matching KV partitions and routes by request priority (<0 -> latency).

Shared weights are required, not an optimisation: weights plus non-torch memory
are 193.75 GiB per GPU of a 267.69 GiB card, so two processes cannot both hold a
copy.

Verified serving on 8xB300 / Kimi-K3 / TP=8: both roles return correct output,
routed by priority, over repeated alternating requests.

Known limits:
  * execution is SEQUENTIAL (latency batch, then throughput); concurrent
    execution across the two streams is not implemented yet
  * requires --no-async-scheduling: the async path uses step_with_batch_queue,
    which is not patched
  * secondary TP group bypasses the custom all-reduce (single shared staging
    buffer would corrupt under two concurrent forwards)

Six defects found by running it, each one layer deeper:
  1. load_model() side effects (decode_query_len) missing on the second runner
  2. KVCacheConfig shallow copy kept full tensor byte sizes -> 2x KV -> OOM
  3. async scheduling bypassed the patched step() entirely
  4. WorkerWrapperBase.execute_model() dropped the role argument
  5. _init_kv_zero_meta() not run for the latency runner
  6. sample_tokens() also needs the role - it is the second half of the same
     forward, so sampling against the other runner found no batch and returned
     None
